"""Displacement, land access and community benefit negotiation."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(101, 'Land Displacement and Community Benefit Negotiation',
        'Land Displacement and\nCommunity Benefit\nNegotiation',
        'Review fictional displacement and benefit proposals for affected uses, procedural fairness and verifiable delivery.', [
        module('Identify displacement and affected uses',
            'Map potential physical and economic displacement and the evidence needed to recognise affected users.',
            """Physical displacement involves relocation or loss of shelter. Economic displacement can arise from losing assets or practical access to resources that support a livelihood. A project may restrict a route, grazing area or market location while every dwelling remains standing. Map each use and how often it occurs before judging the scale of change. In a real case, qualified teams establish land interests, notice requirements and entitlements. Social workers can document access and participation questions and refer legal decisions to the appropriate authority. [S85]

Map uses that can be absent from a land register. Include renters, informal traders, tenants, seasonal farmers and people who gather or process resources. Record whether uses are documented, reported or contested. A person may depend on an asset without owning it or being eligible under the same rules as an owner. Receiving communities can also face changed service demand or altered use of shared land. Ask how to confirm each relevant use without exposing individuals who fear reprisals. [S85]

The Ghana study of perceived ecosystem service loss draws attention to cultural and relational attachments alongside work and income. Residents may depend on places for shared practices or obligations that a price table does not describe. In the case, document the meaning of a proposed loss with the affected groups. Offer ways to record sensitive information without public disclosure. Describe what remains uncertain and who can help interpret the implications. [R99]

Compare options that avoid or minimise displacement before detailing compensation or benefits. Keep a separate line for each unresolved claim or access burden. Record the next review date and how people can ask for corrections in the affected-use record.""",
            'Nobody is moving home, but a fenced corridor cuts off seasonal growers and changes an informal market location.',
            'Identify physical or economic displacement in the case, map users missing from the ownership list and specify evidence and referral roles.',
            'Show how the proposed restriction could remove income-generating access. Include seasonal and informal uses, and reserve entitlement decisions for qualified review.',
            """
Distinguish displacement types. | A project restricts access to a market without moving any household. Which potential effect should be assessed? | Economic displacement through lost trading access. | Physical relocation of all traders. | Labour displacement only for registered employees. | No displacement concern if every house remains. | Losing livelihood access can occur without loss of shelter.
Identify informal uses. | The title register lists owners, and workers report relying on seasonal plots. What should the record include? | The seasonal reliance and how it can be verified. | Only the owner listed in the register. | An assumption that all reports establish eligibility. | A public list of everyone who disputes title. | Affected-use records document dependence without deciding legal interests.
Recognise receiving communities. | Relocated families would use a nearby clinic that is already busy. What should be reviewed? | Service capacity and effects on arriving and existing users. | Only the payment made to departing households. | The project office’s current patient list. | No clinic effect until the entire move ends. | Receiving communities can share or absorb burdens.
Document cultural ties. | An informal route serves a culturally significant place. What belongs in the options assessment? | A protected, community-informed description of access and possible effects. | A fixed monetary value chosen without consultation. | Complete publication of sensitive site details. | Omission because the use yields little direct income. | Cultural loss requires consideration and safe handling.
Refer authority. | Two groups assert overlapping land interests. What is the practitioner’s appropriate role? | Record the dispute and refer entitlement determination to qualified authority. | Decide legal ownership in an open meeting. | Use majority attendance as a title test. | Confirm the operator’s preferred claim without review. | The practitioner documents effects and participation without ruling on legal title.
"""),
        module('Separate negotiated benefits from remedy',
            'Compare a land or benefit proposal with the underlying duties, alternatives and participation conditions.',
            """Begin by naming what each proposal addresses. Compensation and restoration respond to eligible harm or loss under applicable duties or commitments. A community benefit might support a broader development objective such as transport, training or accessible public facilities. Record these categories separately because a promised public benefit may reach people who experience no displacement, while particular households still need an effective response to loss. Confirm actual obligations and decision rights with qualified teams. [S85]

The conditions of negotiation influence what a community can evaluate and challenge. Arhin’s research compares responses in two Ghanaian communities facing displacement from one lithium project. It provides a reason to examine local bargaining power and access to representation. Ask who can obtain independent advice, how representatives were selected and what groups can do when they disagree. Share options before the meeting in accessible formats. Leave time and a review route for people to understand proposed obligations and outcomes. [R95]

A commitment record should identify the responsible party, intended users, start date and steps from promise to delivery. Clarify who will pay operating costs and how use can be verified. A promised bus service can be physically available but scheduled after workers need it. State how the negotiation will deal with changes, unmet commitments and access complaints. Record provisional agreement as provisional. Confirm permissions and local process before taking any formal action. [S89]

Summarise the proposed choices in language residents can use. Explain which need independent or formal review and what each depends on. Ask affected people to check whether their priorities and unresolved questions are represented accurately in the summary.""",
            'The operator offers a training hall for the whole district and suggests counting it as the response for vendors losing their trading space.',
            'Create separate benefit and remedy entries. Specify participation safeguards, an operating condition and who reviews unmet commitments.',
            'Assess lost trading access independently. Verify operator commitments for the hall, access and staffing, and support independent advice and objections.',
            """
Separate purposes. | A communal hall is offered when a vendor group loses its market site. How should the proposal be recorded? | Evaluate the hall benefit and the vendors’ loss and remedy separately. | Treat all losses as settled when the hall is announced. | Divide the hall’s cost among vendors as confirmed compensation. | Record the vendor concern as a construction invoice. | Broader benefits and remedies have different purposes and recipients.
Check representative mandate. | An operator invites one person to speak for all users of shared land. What should be checked? | How the person was selected and how affected groups can participate independently. | The person’s familiarity with the operator alone. | Whether the meeting ends on time. | How many invitations fit in the room. | Representation requires a mandate and an opportunity for omitted groups to contribute.
Enable independent advice. | A group receives a lengthy benefit proposal shortly before signing is requested. What adjustment supports informed participation? | Accessible materials, realistic time and access to independent advice. | Sign simultaneously and review the terms later. | Read only the headline cost in the meeting. | Treat no response as understanding. | People need a practical opportunity to understand and question terms.
Distinguish proposed from binding. | Negotiators note that running costs still need approval. How should the proposed transport benefit be labelled? | Conditional, with a decision owner and deadline. | Delivered as a continuing service. | Guaranteed from the date it was suggested. | Paid automatically when the minutes are filed. | Records must reflect unresolved funding and authority.
Verify service delivery. | A benefit agreement includes a bus. What evidence shows practical benefit for shifts starting early? | Users can board at accessible stops at the required times. | A vehicle is parked near the site. | The agreement names a supplier. | The public timetable uses the same colour as the project logo. | Use shows whether the service actually meets the need.
"""),
        module('Review restoration and disputed commitments',
            'Write a follow-up plan that detects shortfalls, preserves disputed claims and supports fair correction.',
            """Start monitoring before affected access changes, when a meaningful baseline can still be recorded. Compare conditions over relevant seasons, for example access at the start of a growing period and sale opportunities after harvest. Track purchase or relocation assistance separately from outcomes. A record that shows every household received a form does not show that affected people gained access to a suitable alternative. Obtain suitable current evidence through qualified and authorised arrangements. [S85]

Distinguish indicators for individuals and for shared services. A community facility may meet its opening schedule while a group harmed by the initial project continues to face hardship. Separate monetary, access and participation measures. A signed transfer can record what the provider completed. The affected person's account can identify barriers after the transfer. Record source and date for both. Use group breakdowns where appropriate and avoid exposing identifiable cases in public summaries. [R99]

Keep disputed entries visible with fair access to review. Explain the basis of a decision, what evidence is missing and whom a person may approach to question it. The Guiding Principles provide a reference for effective grievance mechanisms, including accessibility, predictable procedure and access to information. An internal route should not obstruct other appropriate remedies. Check whether reliance on the same decision-maker makes a review ineffective, and use an independent route where required or appropriate. [S86]

Use a closure decision that requires a criterion, evidence and a responsible role. If conditions have only partly improved, record which commitments remain open. If further action is needed, agree a review date and verify that people receive an understandable account of the next step.""",
            'All vendor payments were processed, but an alternative market opens late and several vendors remain below baseline net earnings.',
            'Separate completed actions and outcomes, specify one monitoring condition and design a review route with a dated corrective step.',
            'Report payments as delivered and recovery as unresolved. Compare suitable trading periods and address the operating hours with a responsible owner.',
            """
Compare like periods. | Which observation is best for judging whether growers recovered seasonal access? | Use and travel burden measured in matching planting or harvest periods. | A year-end expense after activity stopped. | Any weekday count regardless of season. | The number of growers told about the new site. | Matching periods avoid judging seasonal recovery by a weak comparison.
Separate actions from outcomes. | All payments were processed but net earnings are below baseline. What does the evidence support? | Payment completion with livelihood recovery still unresolved. | Full restoration because accounting finished. | Automatic failure of every payment transaction. | A finding that every household rejected assistance. | Payments and recovery answer different questions.
Retain disputes. | One household contests an outcome and the case manager disagrees. What is a fair next step? | Record the disputed reasoning and explain the applicable review route. | Remove the entry because the case manager responded. | Require disclosure of the complaint to the entire district. | Count the matter as independently reviewed. | Disputed records and usable review help prevent arbitrary closure.
Check independence. | A dispute review would be decided by the person whose action is challenged. What should the team examine? | An appropriate independent route and conflict handling. | Faster review by the same person regardless of impartiality. | Whether the complainant can prove motives before being heard. | Use of the operator’s budget as a decision rule. | A review should be credible and free from avoidable conflict.
Set a closure condition. | Which record best supports marking an access commitment complete? | Specified evidence shows affected users can access the alternative under agreed conditions. | A page states the file is archived. | A comparison uses figures from different populations without explanation. | The project held a closing event. | Completion depends on verified delivery against the agreed criterion.
"""),
    ])
